# Trivy suppressions.
#
# Rules for this file:
#   1. Every entry needs a reason, an owner and a review date. An undated
#      suppression is permanent by accident, and permanent suppressions are how
#      scanners quietly stop finding things.
#   2. Suppress only what you have triaged. "It is noisy" is not triage.
#   3. Prefer fixing. A suppression accepts risk, and someone owns that.
#
# Why these are listed individually rather than excluding the whole directory:
# a path exclusion would also hide any NEW vulnerability that appears in those
# same files. Listing CVEs by id means the 12 known ones are accepted and the
# 13th still breaks the build.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACCEPTED RISK: dependencies of the deliberately vulnerable starter app
#
# Path      : task-1-workload-hardening/app-source/app/requirements.txt
# Owner     : Jubeth S B
# Accepted  : 2026-07-28
# Review by : 2026-10-28, or immediately if the Task 4 pen test concludes
#
# All 12 have fixes available, so under the normal policy (block on fixable
# CRITICAL/HIGH) they would stop the build, and correctly so.
#
# They are accepted because they cannot be fixed without destroying the
# assignment. app-source/ is the authorised target for Task 4's penetration
# test. Upgrading these pins removes the vulnerabilities the pen test exists to
# find, and Flask 0.12.2 / PyYAML 5.1 cannot be upgraded without also replacing
# the interpreter and rewriting the app.
#
# CVE-2019-20477 is worth singling out: "PyYAML: command execution through
# python/object/apply" is precisely the bug app.py exposes at /import via
# yaml.load() without SafeLoader. The scanner independently found the thing the
# pen test is going to exploit, which is a good sign about the scanner.
#
# Compensating controls, from Task 1, that constrain exploitation:
#   - runs as uid 10001, never root
#   - read-only root filesystem, so no webshell or persistence on disk
#   - all Linux capabilities dropped, seccomp RuntimeDefault
#   - no ServiceAccount token mounted, so no pivot to the API server
#   - Kyverno and Pod Security Standards reject any pod that weakens the above
#
# Real remediation, when the pen test is done: port to Python 3.12 with
# Flask 3.x, PyYAML 6.x and requests 2.32.x behind gunicorn. That closes all 12
# and removes the EOL base image at the same time.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Flask 0.12.2
CVE-2018-1000656
CVE-2019-1010083
CVE-2023-30861

# Jinja2 2.10 (sandbox escape via str.format_map)
CVE-2019-10906

# PyYAML 5.1 (CRITICAL, arbitrary command execution). CVE-2019-20477 is the
# /import RCE that Task 4 targets.
CVE-2019-20477
CVE-2020-14343
CVE-2020-1747

# Werkzeug 0.14.1 (path traversal, weak debugger PIN, multipart DoS,
# debugger RCE)
CVE-2019-14322
CVE-2019-14806
CVE-2023-25577
CVE-2024-34069

# requests 2.19.1 (Authorization header leaked across a redirect to a
# different host)
CVE-2018-18074

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACCEPTED RISK: Python packages contributed by the base image
#
# Owner     : Jubeth S B
# Accepted  : 2026-07-28
# Review by : 2026-10-28, or when the base image moves
#
# These are not in requirements.txt. python:3.6-slim ships setuptools, wheel
# and pip preinstalled, and urllib3 arrives as a transitive dependency of the
# pinned requests 2.19.1.
#
# They surfaced because the image gate blocks on `library` findings, which was
# meant to separate "dependencies this repo controls" from "the EOL base image".
# That split turned out to be imperfect: the base contributes both Debian
# packages (vuln-type os) AND Python packages (vuln-type library), so filtering
# by type does not cleanly express the distinction I actually wanted.
#
# Fixing them means either rebuilding the base image or upgrading requests,
# and requests cannot move without moving Flask and the interpreter, which is
# the same application rewrite blocked until the Task 4 pen test concludes.
#
# Listed by id rather than widening the filter, for the same reason as above: a
# new vulnerability in these packages should still break the build.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# setuptools (base image): path traversal, RCE via package_index download
CVE-2022-40897
CVE-2024-6345
CVE-2025-47273

# wheel (base image): DoS via crafted input
CVE-2022-40898

# urllib3 (transitive via requests 2.19.1): certificate handling, cookie leak
# on cross-origin redirect, decompression-bomb bypass, header disclosure
CVE-2019-11324
CVE-2023-43804
CVE-2025-66471
CVE-2026-21441
CVE-2026-44431
